description:
pkix-lint/src/oscal/parse.rs (entire file) — The two public parsers `deviation_store_from_risks` and `lint_ids_from_catalog` (and the helper `resolve_profile` in oscal/profile.rs) do not inspect the input's metadata.oscal-version field.

The OSCAL_VERSION constant ("1.1.2") is defined in two places — oscal/emit.rs:80 and oscal/catalog.rs:81 — and used only on the emit side. The parse side accepts JSON conforming to ANY oscal-version, parses against the 1.1.2 schema shape, and produces a DeviationStore / Vec<String> that may silently lose, mis-interpret, or panic on fields that have different semantics in different schema versions.

CALLER IMPACT: An operator producing OSCAL via a tool that targets OSCAL 1.0.4 (older) or 1.2.0 (newer) gets one of:
- Silent data loss (fields the parser doesn't understand in the wrong-version layout are ignored).
- Wrong data ingest (fields with renamed paths produce ParseError::MissingField or ParseError::WrongType for the wrong-version input).
- Worst case: silent acceptance of data with subtly different semantics, producing a DeviationStore that is structurally valid but semantically wrong.

A round-trip emit-then-parse always works because the emitter writes 1.1.2. A round-trip parse-then-emit silently re-stamps the data as 1.1.2 regardless of the input's claimed version.

REMEDIATION: Add a version check at parse entry. Two design options:

1. Strict accept-1.1.2-only:
   pub enum ParseError {
       ...
       #[error("unsupported OSCAL version: found {found:?}, expected 1.1.2")]
       UnsupportedOscalVersion { found: String },
   }
   At the start of each parse function:
       let version = value
           .get("metadata").and_then(|m| m.get("oscal-version"))
           .and_then(|v| v.as_str())
           .ok_or(ParseError::MissingOscalVersion)?;
       if version != crate::oscal::OSCAL_VERSION {
           return Err(ParseError::UnsupportedOscalVersion {
               found: version.to_string(),
           });
       }

2. Allowlist with explicit compat range:
   const SUPPORTED_OSCAL_VERSIONS: &[&str] = &["1.1.2"];  // expand as needed
   if !SUPPORTED_OSCAL_VERSIONS.contains(&version) {
       return Err(ParseError::UnsupportedOscalVersion { found: ... });
   }

Document the supported-version set in the oscal/mod.rs module rustdoc so callers know what to target.

RELATED: oscal/emit.rs:80 and oscal/catalog.rs:81 duplicate the OSCAL_VERSION constant. The duplication is a separate-but-related concern — see related finding (PKIX-7f92.17 already flags parallel-but-separate uuid_v8 namespaces; same pattern of duplication). Consolidate the version constant into oscal/mod.rs as `pub(crate) const OSCAL_VERSION: &str = "1.1.2";` (or `pub` if callers should be able to inspect it programmatically) and reference it from both emit and parse.
